2026-09-04T05:32Z GAP C RE-DERIVED AGAINST THE REVIEWED BASELINE (b0e0bc8/v1.13.0, fork/ rebuilt via make upstream-checkout + npm ci + npm run build). Confirms the adapter docstring's three claims by source, not by its own argument, and finds a fourth thing the ticket did not have. (a) stop's cancel flag really is in-memory only: requestStop() (index-manager.ts:742-746) sets this.cancelToken.cancelled=true on a plain object, nothing else. (b) the checkpoint really reaches disk, unconditionally, on cancellation: buildIncremental's exit (index-manager.ts:1370-1381) calls noteCheckpoint() then awaits persistNow() on both the cancelled and completed path; persist() is this.save() (line 1012), which for sqlite 'commits its open transaction here' (line 1010-1011); the checkpoint lives in the same file, meta table, key 'checkpoint' (sqlite-index.ts:928, :1092). (c) build really is the only function that continues an interrupted build, and it really cannot be separated from the destructive branches: resumeFrom() (index-manager.ts:871-880) returns the checkpoint unless opts.fresh, and fresh is set only by action:'refresh' (build.ts:123) -- but action:'update' ALSO resumes when it must: updateBlocker() (index-manager.ts:1409-1422) refuses a delta when a checkpoint exists with no stamp and says outright the full build 'RESUMES that one rather than starting over'; startIndexUpdate (build.ts:472-480) answers that refusal by calling startIndexBuild, the identical function action:'build' calls -- no third function exists. repairSearchIndex (repair.ts) runs inside that same RPC, before buildIncremental, whenever the store is faulted (index-tool.ts:96-106) -- true of action:'refresh' too, since the fault-check only special-cases 'update' (index-tool.ts:82-95), which repair.ts's own comment ('Only ever called for an explicit action:build', repair.ts:15) does not quite say -- a minor doc/code gap, not load-bearing since refresh already discards on purpose. So a target with resume mapped onto build could not be resume-only even in principle: the same RPC silently repairs when the store is unreadable, state the caller does not control ex ante. Confirmed, not merely argued.
